% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% What a page carries so it can be read back (design 14.5)
%
% Three marks, and the reason for each:
%
%   \runseed   the run this sheet belongs to, printed after the version
%              as `v715-1513199331`. A loose sheet can then be traced to
%              its run by eye, with no equipment.
%   \cipsid    the student id, when the run prints names. Blank runs
%              leave it unset and the header prints empty boxes to write
%              in -- which is what lets an unnamed spare be graded.
%   the code   everything above plus the skill and the sheet number, as
%              a QR in the footer, so splitting a scanned stack needs no
%              OCR at all.
%
% `qrcode` is pure TeX and draws with no external tool, which matters:
% a build must not depend on something the instructor has to install.
\usepackage{qrcode}

\newcommand{\runseed}{0}
% Also stamps the page index, because this is the moment the run seed
% becomes known -- the stream is opened when the package loads, which
% is before the document body has said which run this is.
\newcommand{\setrunseed}[1]{%
  \renewcommand{\runseed}{#1}%
  \immediate\write\cipindex{run*#1}}

% Either mark can be turned off for a run. Both default **on**: a
% document that says nothing about them is an older one, and printing
% less than it asked for is the worse way to be wrong.
%
% `\ifcipid` covers the whole id field, label included -- a bare
% "ID:" with nothing after it is worse than no id at all.
\newif\ifcipid\cipidtrue
\newif\ifcipcodeon\cipcodeontrue

\newcommand{\cipsid}{}
\newcommand{\setsid}[1]{\renewcommand{\cipsid}{#1}}

% Which sheet of the current skill this is. Reset by \skillheader,
% which runs once at the top of every skill, and stepped in the footer,
% which is expanded once per page at shipout and therefore in order.
\newcounter{cipsheet}
\setcounter{cipsheet}{0}

% What kind of paper this is. A scanned stack may contain all three and
% they are graded differently -- a key must never be recorded as an
% attempt, and a spare belongs to nobody until someone writes an id on
% it. Nothing on the page says which, so the document has to.
\newcommand{\cipkind}{student}
\newcommand{\setkind}[1]{\renewcommand{\cipkind}{#1}}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% The page index: what the code's key actually resolves to.
%
% The footer code says `C1-<runseed>-<page>` and nothing else, on the
% stated grounds that run plus page is a unique key and everything else
% is "a lookup in that run's manifest". It was not: `manifest.toml`
% records which (skill, version, seed) were *drawn*, with no students
% and no page numbers in it, and the print record's `printed` table has
% a row per student per paper with no page either. So the key was real
% and the table it keyed into did not exist.
%
% Only one party knows which page a given paper landed on, and it is
% not Python: a skill can run to two sheets, so page numbers exist only
% once TeX has broken the pages. The document therefore writes the
% table itself, one line per page, as it ships them.
%
%   run*<runseed>
%   <page>*<kind>*<sid>*<slug>*<vseed>*<sheet>
%
% `*` separates, as it did in the first payload, because `-` occurs in
% slugs. The header's first field is `run` rather than a page number,
% which no page can collide with -- and avoids `#`, which `\write`
% would double.
%
% An absent sid is empty: a blank-name run prints boxes to write one
% into, and claiming an owner we do not have would be worse than
% admitting we have none.
%
% Written from the footer, not from a shipout hook, and that is the
% whole trick for blank pages: \preparefornextstudent pads with
% \pagestyle{empty}, which has no footer, so a padding page writes no
% line without anyone having to detect one. The gaps in the sequence
% are the blanks.
%
% No space before any `*`: a control sequence name ends at the first
% non-letter, so `\thepage*` is already two tokens.
\newwrite\cipindex
\immediate\openout\cipindex=\jobname.pages\relax
\newcommand{\cipwriteindex}{%
  \immediate\write\cipindex{%
    \thepage*\cipkind*\cipsid*\currentskill*\vseed*\thecipsheet}}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Where the grading box is, in the page's own coordinates.
%
%   box*<page>*<x>*<y>*<width>*<height>*<depth>
%
% All six numbers in scaled points, from the bottom-left of the page.
% Scans has to crop the box out of a scan and read what is in it, and
% there were three ways to know where it is:
%
%   - compute it from the footer's geometry, which breaks silently the
%     next time anyone adjusts the footer;
%   - measure it off a render, which is the same constant with an extra
%     step in front of it, and this project has a bad record with
%     measurement harnesses;
%   - ask the typesetter, which is where the answer actually lives.
%
% \pdfsavepos records the position of the point it sits at, and
% \pdflastxpos is set when the page is shipped -- so the \write has to
% be deferred, not \immediate, or it reads the previous page's. The
% box is measured into a save box first, which makes the rectangle
% exact rather than inferred: x to x+width, y-depth to y+height.
%
% Only first sheets write one. The continuation style deliberately
% omits the box -- one skill is ticked once -- so a continued sheet
% records no box, and "no box on this page" is a fact Scans wants
% anyway.
% `\global`, and it is not optional. The footer is built inside a
% group, the \write above is deferred, and a deferred write runs at
% shipout -- after that group has closed. A locally set save box is
% void again by then, and the first version of this recorded a box
% 0sp wide at exactly the right place.
% The box is measured into a save box and then drawn *separately*,
% rather than the measured copy being used. `\sbox` typesets without
% contributing anything, so the measurement is free of consequences and
% the drawn box is the same tokens the footer always had.
%
% Using the measured copy changed the page. `\roundbox` ends in
% `\xspace`, which decides whether to add a space by looking at what
% comes next -- and inside a save box what comes next is the end of the
% box, not the end of the footer. The box and the skill slug beside it
% shifted about 0.8mm, found by differencing a page against one built
% with this morning's theme: 3,344 pixels out of 8.4 million, all of
% them in that corner.
\newsavebox{\cipboxmeasure}
\newcommand{\cipcheckbox}[1]{%
  \global\sbox{\cipboxmeasure}{\roundbox{#1}}%
  \pdfsavepos
  \write\cipindex{box*\thepage*\the\pdflastxpos*\the\pdflastypos*%
    \number\wd\cipboxmeasure*\number\ht\cipboxmeasure*%
    \number\dp\cipboxmeasure}%
  \roundbox{#1}}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% The payload: the least that identifies a sheet.
%
%   C1-1513199331-347
%
% **`runseed` plus `page` is already a unique key.** A run numbers its
% pages once, so the skill, the version seed, the sheet-within-skill
% and the student are every one of them a lookup in that run's
% manifest. Putting any of them in the code buys nothing a scanner
% needs and costs modules.
%
% `C1` is the format version, and is nearly free: 17 characters still
% fits a version-1 symbol, which holds 20 bytes at level M. Without it
% a later change of fields would be unreadable rather than merely
% different.
%
% Seventeen characters is 21 modules. The 47-character feedback URL
% this replaced was 33 -- and because a symbol's physical size is set
% by its module count, that is the whole difference between a 15mm
% square and an 11mm one at the same scan quality. A code that also
% took students to feedback was simply not worth 4mm of paper.
%
% What is given up: a loose sheet whose run folder is gone says
% nothing about itself. The skill is printed beside the code in words
% for exactly that case.
\newcommand{\cippayload}{C1-\runseed-\thepage}

