% What the footer calls, once per page. Three jobs in a fixed order,
% and the order is the point: the sheet is counted and the index line
% written on every page that has a footer, whether or not the code is
% drawn on it.
%
% Keeping the counter and the index inside `\ifcipcodeon` would have
% tied the record of what was printed to the decision to print a
% symbol -- two unrelated things, and a page index that silently
% emptied itself when someone turned the codes off is a worse failure
% than having no index at all, because it looks like a run with no
% pages.
%
% One call per page is guaranteed by fancyhdr, not by us: `\cipcode`
% sits in [LO] and [RE], and exactly one of those fires per page.
\newcommand{\cippagemark}{%
  \stepcounter{cipsheet}%
  \cipwriteindex
  \cipcode}

% Drawn small, and quiet: this is for a scanner, and a student should
% not feel watched by it.
\newcommand{\cipcode}{%
  % The switch lives here rather than at the four call sites -- two
  % page styles times two parities -- so there is one copy of the
  % decision and it cannot fall out of step with itself.
  \ifcipcodeon
  % `nolink`, which is why it was cyan. `qrcode` sets
  % `\qr@hyperlinktrue` by default, wraps the code in a hyperref
  % link, and hyperref colours it -- so wrapping it in
  % `\textcolor{black}` changed nothing, because the colour was not
  % coming from the surrounding text. A payload of `CIP1*...` is not a
  % URL either, so the link was never meaningful.
  %
  % A scanner wants maximum contrast; light cyan on white is close to
  % the worst case a decoder can be handed.
  %
  % 9mm, not 7.5: 33 modules across 7.5mm is 0.23mm each, which a
  % 300dpi scan samples at under three pixels. Three is the floor
  % every decoder quotes, so it was sitting exactly on it.
  % 11mm at level **L**, which is a deliberate pair and not two
  % independent settings.
  %
  % A version-1 symbol is 21 modules and holds 17 bytes at level L
  % but only 14 at level M. The payload is exactly 17. At M it
  % therefore spills to version 2 -- 25 modules -- and since the
  % height is fixed, more modules means each one is *smaller*:
  % measured at 11mm/M it was 0.423mm, 3.33 pixels at a 200dpi scan.
  % At L the same 11mm holds 21 modules at 0.52mm, 4.1 pixels.
  %
  % So L is not a concession here. It buys a coarser symbol, and a
  % coarser symbol survives a worse scan than a finer one with more
  % error correction in it. What is given up is damage tolerance: 7%
  % of the symbol may be lost rather than 15%. In a footer corner,
  % where staples and folds live, that is the real cost.
  %
  % If the payload ever grows past 17 characters this silently falls
  % to version 2 and the modules shrink again. Re-measure after any
  % change to `\cippayload`.
  %
  % Sizes here are measured off a 600dpi render, never computed from
  % the QR version tables -- `qrcode` puts its quiet zone inside the
  % height it is given, and the tables were misread once already.
  %
  % Centred on the footer text rather than hung below it: `\raisebox`
  % by half the height, less half the x-height, puts the middle of the
  % square on the middle of the line.
  % Centred the way `\roundbox` is, and by the same mechanism: a
  % tikzpicture with `baseline=-0.7ex`, which puts the picture's
  % origin 0.7ex above the text baseline and the node's centre on it.
  % Two nested `\raisebox`es were doing this arithmetically and still
  % reading as top-aligned; copying the thing already in the footer
  % that looks right is better than computing something that should.
  \begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=-0.7ex]
    \node[inner sep=0pt, outer sep=0pt] {\textcolor{black}{%
      \qrcode[height=11mm, level=L, nolink]{\cippayload}}};
  \end{tikzpicture}%
  \fi}

